window.QBANK={"1":[{"i":"AUD-III-A-0001","t":"The auditor of Riverbend Wholesale, a nonissuer, receives a system-generated accounts receivable aging report and plans to use it to select balances for confirmation. Which procedure best addresses the reliability of the report before it is used?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Riverbend Wholesale, a nonissuer, receives a system-generated accounts receivable aging report and plans to use it to select balances for confirmation. Which procedure best addresses the reliability of the report before it is used?","d":"","o":["Reconcile its total to the general ledger and test the controls over its preparation, to establish accuracy and completeness","Ask management to certify in writing that the report is accurate and use it without further procedures","Rely on the report because it was produced by the entity's accounting system rather than being prepared manually by a person","Test only the ten largest balances on the report for existence"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 500.08b; AU-C 500.A40-.A41","rat":"The auditor evaluates whether information is accurate and complete, for example by reconciling it and testing controls over its preparation.","ex":["The auditor evaluates whether information is accurate and complete, for example by reconciling it and testing controls over its preparation.","A representation is not a substitute for evidence about the accuracy and completeness of the information.","System output can be inaccurate or incomplete, and favoring automated output without evaluation is a bias.","Testing existence of the largest balances says nothing about whether the report is complete."],"wt":["","trap","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Evaluate the accuracy and completeness of information the entity produces before using it as audit evidence.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"2":[{"i":"AUD-III-A-0002","t":"The auditor of Summit Rail, a nonissuer, asks the IT department for an extract of all 2025 purchase transactions to test for duplicate payments with data analytics. Which step most directly supports the completeness of the extracted population?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Summit Rail, a nonissuer, asks the IT department for an extract of all 2025 purchase transactions to test for duplicate payments with data analytics. Which step most directly supports the completeness of the extracted population?","d":"","o":["Obtain an email from the IT department stating that the extract includes all transactions","Sort the extract by vendor name and remove rows with missing vendor numbers before running the analytics","Select a sample of vendors from the extract and confirm that they exist","Reconcile the record count and control totals of the extract to the general ledger and subledger totals"],"a":4,"cite":"AU-C 500.A40-.A41","rat":"Reconciling counts and totals to the accounting records tests whether the population is complete.","ex":["An assertion from IT is not evidence about completeness of the extract.","Removing rows before analysis can make the population less complete, not more.","Confirming existence of vendors addresses validity, not whether all transactions were extracted.","Reconciling counts and totals to the accounting records tests whether the population is complete."],"wt":["trap","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Reconcile extracted data to the accounting records to establish completeness before running analytics.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"3":[{"i":"AUD-III-A-0003","t":"Using an analytics tool, the auditor of Coral Bay Resorts, a nonissuer, isolates 412 of 38,000 journal entries that were posted on weekends by users with no accounting role. Which use of the results is most appropriate?","mc":{"s":"Using an analytics tool, the auditor of Coral Bay Resorts, a nonissuer, isolates 412 of 38,000 journal entries that were posted on weekends by users with no accounting role. Which use of the results is most appropriate?","d":"","o":["Conclude that the 38,000 unflagged entries are appropriate because the tool examined the entire population","Treat all 412 flagged entries as errors and propose adjustments for each one","Select 25 of the 412 at random and project the results to all 38,000 entries","Investigate the flagged entries for purpose and authorization, and use the results in assessing override risk"],"a":4,"cite":"AU-C 240.32a; AU-C 500.A12c","rat":"Flagged entries call for investigation of purpose and authorization, and the results inform the override risk assessment.","ex":["The tool applied specific criteria; entries that pass those criteria are not shown to be appropriate.","Flagged entries are unusual, not necessarily wrong, so the auditor investigates before concluding.","Results from the flagged group cannot be projected to a population that was not sampled from.","Flagged entries call for investigation of purpose and authorization, and the results inform the override risk assessment."],"wt":["trap","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Analytics flags items for investigation; the auditor evaluates the flagged items and does not treat the unflagged population as cleared.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"4":[{"i":"AUD-III-A-0004","t":"Which set of attributes affects the reliability of information used as audit evidence under AICPA standards?","mc":{"s":"Which set of attributes affects the reliability of information used as audit evidence under AICPA standards?","d":"","o":["Materiality, timeliness, cost and convenience of obtaining the information in question","Accuracy, completeness, authenticity and susceptibility to management bias","Persuasiveness, sufficiency, precision and volume","Size, format, storage location and age of the record"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 500.A27","rat":"Reliability is affected, to varying degrees, by accuracy, completeness, authenticity and susceptibility to management bias.","ex":["Cost and convenience are not attributes of reliability.","Reliability is affected, to varying degrees, by accuracy, completeness, authenticity and susceptibility to management bias.","Sufficiency concerns the quantity of evidence rather than the reliability of information.","Format and location may matter for procedures but are not the listed attributes."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Reliability depends on accuracy, completeness, authenticity and susceptibility to management bias.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"5":[{"i":"AUD-III-B-0001","t":"In auditing the existence of cash at Ardent Fabrics, a nonissuer, the auditor considers four sources of evidence. Which is most reliable?","mc":{"s":"In auditing the existence of cash at Ardent Fabrics, a nonissuer, the auditor considers four sources of evidence. Which is most reliable?","d":"","o":["A bank confirmation the auditor receives directly from the bank","A bank statement retained in the treasurer's files and provided to the auditor","Management's oral explanation of the year-end cash position","A photocopy of a bank reconciliation prepared by client staff"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 500.A22-.A24; PCAOB AS 1105.08","rat":"Evidence from an external party obtained directly by the auditor is generally the most reliable.","ex":["Evidence from an external party obtained directly by the auditor is generally the most reliable.","A document retained by the client and provided by management is internal and obtained indirectly.","Oral representations are generally less reliable than documentary evidence.","A client-prepared reconciliation is internal evidence, and a photocopy is less reliable than an original."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Reliability is higher for external sources, direct acquisition by the auditor, and documentary forms.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"6":[{"i":"AUD-III-B-0002","t":"Which statement correctly describes the sufficiency and appropriateness of audit evidence?","mc":{"s":"Which statement correctly describes the sufficiency and appropriateness of audit evidence?","d":"","o":["Sufficiency measures the reliability of evidence, and appropriateness measures the number of items tested.","Sufficiency and appropriateness are the same measure of evidence, described with different terms by different standards.","Sufficiency measures quantity of evidence, and appropriateness measures its quality, including relevance and reliability.","A larger quantity of evidence can compensate for evidence of poor quality, provided the total is substantial."],"a":3,"cite":"PCAOB AS 1105.05-.06; AU-C 500.05","rat":"Sufficiency concerns quantity and appropriateness concerns quality, which includes relevance and reliability.","ex":["The definitions are reversed.","They are distinct: one is quantity and the other is quality.","Sufficiency concerns quantity and appropriateness concerns quality, which includes relevance and reliability.","Quantity does not make up for poor quality; the amount needed depends on risk and the quality of the evidence."],"wt":["concept","concept","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Sufficiency is quantity; appropriateness is quality (relevance and reliability).","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"7":[{"i":"AUD-III-B-0003","t":"The auditor of Marsh Energy, a nonissuer, receives a supplier confirmation showing a payable of $420,000, while the company's accounts payable subledger shows $310,000. Management says the difference is due to an invoice that has not been recorded yet. What should the auditor do?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Marsh Energy, a nonissuer, receives a supplier confirmation showing a payable of $420,000, while the company's accounts payable subledger shows $310,000. Management says the difference is due to an invoice that has not been recorded yet. What should the auditor do?","d":"","o":["Determine whether modified or additional procedures are needed to resolve the difference, such as examining the unrecorded invoice","Accept the subledger balance, because management knows the supplier relationship and the timing of its invoices better than the supplier's confirmation shows","Average the confirmed and recorded amounts to estimate the payable","Disregard the confirmation, because it conflicts with the entity's own records"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 500.10","rat":"Inconsistent evidence from two sources requires the auditor to determine what modified or additional procedures will resolve it.","ex":["Inconsistent evidence from two sources requires the auditor to determine what modified or additional procedures will resolve it.","Management's explanation is uncorroborated and cannot outweigh contradictory evidence from an external party.","Averaging does not resolve the difference or determine which amount is right.","Contradictory external evidence cannot be disregarded because it disagrees with internal records; it points to possible unrecorded liabilities."],"wt":["","trap","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Evaluation","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Resolve inconsistencies in audit evidence with modified or additional procedures.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"8":[{"i":"AUD-III-B-0004","t":"An auditor of an issuer plans to use a company-produced inventory listing as audit evidence. What do PCAOB standards require the auditor to do?","mc":{"s":"An auditor of an issuer plans to use a company-produced inventory listing as audit evidence. What do PCAOB standards require the auditor to do?","d":"","o":["Test the completeness of the listing only, because accuracy is presumed for electronically produced company data","Nothing further, provided the listing was produced by the company's ERP system and reconciles to the general ledger total","Obtain a written management representation about the accuracy of the listing and the completeness of the items on it","Test the accuracy and completeness of the information, or the controls over them, and evaluate its precision and detail"],"a":4,"cite":"PCAOB AS 1105.10","rat":"The auditor must test accuracy and completeness, or the controls over them, and evaluate whether the information is sufficiently precise and detailed.","ex":["Both accuracy and completeness must be addressed.","System production does not remove the requirement to evaluate the information.","A representation does not replace testing accuracy and completeness or the controls over them.","The auditor must test accuracy and completeness, or the controls over them, and evaluate whether the information is sufficiently precise and detailed."],"wt":["concept","trap","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"For company-produced information, test accuracy and completeness (or controls over them) and evaluate precision.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"9":[{"i":"AUD-III-C-0001","t":"An auditor designs a test of controls over credit approvals at Fenwick Retail, a nonissuer. Holding other factors constant, which change would increase the required sample size?","mc":{"s":"An auditor designs a test of controls over credit approvals at Fenwick Retail, a nonissuer. Holding other factors constant, which change would increase the required sample size?","d":"","o":["A higher acceptable risk of overreliance","A decrease in the tolerable rate of deviation","A decrease in the expected rate of deviation","An increase in the tolerable rate of deviation"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 530.A12-.A13","rat":"A lower tolerable rate of deviation requires more evidence and a larger sample.","ex":["Accepting a higher risk of overreliance means less assurance is needed, so the sample can be smaller.","A lower tolerable rate of deviation requires more evidence and a larger sample.","A lower expected deviation rate reduces the sample size.","A higher tolerable rate requires less precision, so the sample can be smaller."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Sample size rises as the tolerable deviation rate falls, the expected deviation rate rises, or the desired assurance rises.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"10":[{"i":"AUD-III-C-0002","t":"The auditor of Larkin Distributors, a nonissuer, tests 80 sales invoices for evidence of credit approval and finds no approval on 6 of them. The tolerable rate of deviation is 5%, and the expected rate was 1%. Which conclusion is correct?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Larkin Distributors, a nonissuer, tests 80 sales invoices for evidence of credit approval and finds no approval on 6 of them. The tolerable rate of deviation is 5%, and the expected rate was 1%. Which conclusion is correct?","d":"","o":["The sample deviation rate is 7.5%, but no conclusion about reliance is possible without a statistical projection to the population.","The sample deviation rate is 1.3%, which is below the tolerable rate, so the planned reliance on the control is supported.","The sample deviation rate is 7.5%, which exceeds the tolerable rate, so the planned reliance on the control is not supported.","The sample deviation rate is 6.0%, which exceeds the tolerable rate, so the planned reliance on the control is not supported."],"a":3,"cite":"AU-C 530.A25; AU-C 330.16","rat":"6 / 80 = 7.5%, and for tests of controls the sample deviation rate is also the projected rate, which exceeds the 5% tolerable rate.","ex":["For tests of controls, the sample deviation rate is the projected deviation rate for the population.","This divides by the wrong base and also ignores that 6 deviations were found, not 1.","6 / 80 = 7.5%, and for tests of controls the sample deviation rate is also the projected rate, which exceeds the 5% tolerable rate.","The rate is computed on 80 items, not 100; 6 / 100 would give 6.0% but the sample is 80."],"wt":["concept","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"For a test of controls, the sample deviation rate is the projected rate and is compared with the tolerable rate.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Sample deviation rate = 6 / 80 = 7.5%","7.5% > tolerable rate 5%, so reliance is not supported"]}}],"11":[{"i":"AUD-III-C-0003","t":"The auditor of Larkin Distributors, a nonissuer, tests accounts receivable using a nonstatistical sample. The population has 1,500 accounts with a recorded amount of $3,000,000. The sample has 100 accounts with a recorded amount of $210,000, and the auditor finds overstatements of $8,400 in the sample. Using the ratio of sample misstatement to sample recorded amount, what is the projected misstatement for the population?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Larkin Distributors, a nonissuer, tests accounts receivable using a nonstatistical sample. The population has 1,500 accounts with a recorded amount of $3,000,000. The sample has 100 accounts with a recorded amount of $210,000, and the auditor finds overstatements of $8,400 in the sample. Using the ratio of sample misstatement to sample recorded amount, what is the projected misstatement for the population?","d":"","o":["$8,400","$84,000","$120,000","$126,000"],"a":3,"cite":"AU-C 530.13; AU-C 530.A24","rat":"$8,400 / $210,000 = 4%, and 4% x $3,000,000 = $120,000.","ex":["This is the misstatement found in the sample, not projected to the population.","This multiplies the sample misstatement by 10, which has no basis in the population-to-sample relationship.","$8,400 / $210,000 = 4%, and 4% x $3,000,000 = $120,000.","This projects the average misstatement per item ($84) over 1,500 accounts, a different method (difference estimation)."],"wt":["calc","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Ratio projection multiplies the population's recorded amount by the ratio of sample misstatement to sample recorded amount.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Misstatement ratio = 8,400 / 210,000 = 4.0%","Projected misstatement = 4.0% x 3,000,000 = 120,000"]}}],"12":[{"i":"AUD-III-C-0004","t":"In the receivables test of Larkin Distributors, a nonissuer, the projected misstatement is $120,000 and tolerable misstatement is $130,000. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","mc":{"s":"In the receivables test of Larkin Distributors, a nonissuer, the projected misstatement is $120,000 and tolerable misstatement is $130,000. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","d":"","o":["The misstatements found in the sample can be ignored, because the sample is small relative to the population","The balance is not materially misstated because $120,000 is below the tolerable misstatement of $130,000, so no further work is needed","The auditor should record $120,000 as an audit adjustment, because a projection is the best measure of the actual misstatement in the balance","Sampling risk is significant because the projection is close to tolerable misstatement; consider extending testing or asking management to adjust"],"a":4,"cite":"AU-C 530.A26-.A27","rat":"When projected misstatement is close to tolerable misstatement, the risk that actual misstatement exceeds it is unacceptable, so more work or correction is considered.","ex":["Misstatements identified in a sample must be investigated, projected and evaluated.","A projection below tolerable misstatement does not by itself give assurance when it is close, because of sampling risk.","Due to sampling risk, a projection may not be sufficient to determine an amount to be recorded.","When projected misstatement is close to tolerable misstatement, the risk that actual misstatement exceeds it is unacceptable, so more work or correction is considered."],"wt":["concept","trap","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Evaluation","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A projected misstatement near tolerable misstatement signals unacceptable sampling risk.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"13":[{"i":"AUD-III-C-0005","t":"The auditor of Brookline Marine, a nonissuer, uses monetary-unit sampling for a receivables population of $2,400,000. Tolerable misstatement is $80,000, the auditor expects no misstatements, and the reliability factor for the desired risk of incorrect acceptance is 3.0. What sample size results, using population divided by the sampling interval, where the interval equals tolerable misstatement divided by the reliability factor?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Brookline Marine, a nonissuer, uses monetary-unit sampling for a receivables population of $2,400,000. Tolerable misstatement is $80,000, the auditor expects no misstatements, and the reliability factor for the desired risk of incorrect acceptance is 3.0. What sample size results, using population divided by the sampling interval, where the interval equals tolerable misstatement divided by the reliability factor?","d":"","o":["30","72","90","26,667"],"a":3,"cite":"AICPA Audit Sampling audit guide","rat":"Interval = $80,000 / 3.0 = $26,667, and sample size = $2,400,000 / $26,667 = 90.","ex":["This divides the population by tolerable misstatement and omits the reliability factor.","This uses a tolerable misstatement of $100,000 rather than $80,000.","Interval = $80,000 / 3.0 = $26,667, and sample size = $2,400,000 / $26,667 = 90.","This is the sampling interval in dollars, not the number of items in the sample."],"wt":["calc","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Monetary-unit sample size equals the population divided by the sampling interval, where interval is tolerable misstatement divided by the reliability factor.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Sampling interval = 80,000 / 3.0 = 26,667","Sample size = 2,400,000 / 26,667 = 90"]}}],"14":[{"i":"AUD-III-C-0006","t":"Which characteristics make a sampling approach statistical under AICPA standards?","mc":{"s":"Which characteristics make a sampling approach statistical under AICPA standards?","d":"","o":["Selection of items that the auditor judges to be representative of the population being tested","Random selection of items plus a statistical technique to evaluate results, including sampling risk","Use of computer software to select the sample items from the population without manual intervention","A sample size that is large enough, such as more than 100 items drawn from the population under test"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 530.05; AU-C 530.A14","rat":"Statistical sampling requires random selection and statistical evaluation of results, including measurement of sampling risk.","ex":["Judgmental selection is nonstatistical because it lacks random selection.","Statistical sampling requires random selection and statistical evaluation of results, including measurement of sampling risk.","Software can select items in a nonstatistical way; the definition is about random selection and statistical evaluation.","Sample size is not a valid criterion for choosing between statistical and nonstatistical approaches."],"wt":["concept","","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Statistical sampling combines random selection with a statistical technique for evaluating results.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"15":[{"i":"AUD-III-C-0007","t":"Oakmont Supply, a nonissuer, has 1,940 receivable balances. Forty balances over $100,000 make up 55% of the total dollar amount, and the rest are small. The auditor wants an efficient and effective substantive test. Which approach is most appropriate?","mc":{"s":"Oakmont Supply, a nonissuer, has 1,940 receivable balances. Forty balances over $100,000 make up 55% of the total dollar amount, and the rest are small. The auditor wants an efficient and effective substantive test. Which approach is most appropriate?","d":"","o":["Examine the 40 large balances separately and select a sample from the remaining small balances","Take a random sample of 60 from the entire population and give the large balances no separate consideration","Select the sample only from the 40 large balances and extrapolate the results to all 1,940","Take a haphazard sample of 60 items from the whole population without regard to size"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 530.A15; AU-C 530.05 (stratification)","rat":"Items of high value or risk can be examined separately and sampling applied to the rest, or the population can be stratified.","ex":["Items of high value or risk can be examined separately and sampling applied to the rest, or the population can be stratified.","A simple random sample could miss most large items, so it may not be an effective response.","Results from the large balances cannot be projected to the small balances that were not sampled.","A sample that ignores size may miss most of the dollars in the large balances."],"wt":["","trap","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Examine high-value items separately, stratify the population, and sample the remainder.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"16":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0001","t":"The auditor of Foxglove Pharma, a nonissuer, plans to rely on a control requiring supervisor approval of purchase orders above $10,000. Which procedure provides the most persuasive evidence of operating effectiveness throughout the period?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Foxglove Pharma, a nonissuer, plans to rely on a control requiring supervisor approval of purchase orders above $10,000. Which procedure provides the most persuasive evidence of operating effectiveness throughout the period?","d":"","o":["Inquire of the purchasing manager about how approvals of purchase orders over $10,000 are performed","Observe a supervisor approving purchase orders on one day during fieldwork","Inspect a sample of purchase orders from throughout the year for evidence of supervisor approval","Read the purchasing policy manual that requires supervisor approval"],"a":3,"cite":"AU-C 330.10a; AU-C 330.11","rat":"Inspecting items from across the period gives evidence of how the control was applied and its consistency, beyond inquiry alone.","ex":["Inquiry alone does not provide sufficient evidence of operating effectiveness.","Observation covers only the time it is performed and does not show the control operated all year.","Inspecting items from across the period gives evidence of how the control was applied and its consistency, beyond inquiry alone.","A policy manual shows what should happen, not that it did happen."],"wt":["trap","trap","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Tests of controls combine inquiry with inspection, observation or reperformance across the period.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"17":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0002","t":"The auditor of Halden Furniture, a nonissuer with a December 31 year-end, tested a control over sales returns approvals at October 31 and found it effective. The auditor plans to rely on the control for the full year. What is required?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Halden Furniture, a nonissuer with a December 31 year-end, tested a control over sales returns approvals at October 31 and found it effective. The auditor plans to rely on the control for the full year. What is required?","d":"","o":["Retest the control for the entire year, because interim evidence has no value","Obtain evidence on changes to the control after October 31 and decide what added evidence the remaining period needs","Obtain a management representation that the control operated as designed until year-end","Nothing further, because a control that was effective at interim is assumed to keep operating effectively until year-end"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 330.12; PCAOB AS 2201.55","rat":"When interim evidence is used, the auditor obtains evidence about significant changes and determines additional evidence for the remaining period.","ex":["Interim evidence can be used with additional evidence for the remaining period; retesting the whole year is not required.","When interim evidence is used, the auditor obtains evidence about significant changes and determines additional evidence for the remaining period.","A representation is not sufficient evidence about operating effectiveness.","Effectiveness at interim does not establish effectiveness for the remaining period."],"wt":["trap","","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Interim control test results are extended to year-end through evidence of changes and procedures for the remaining period.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"18":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0003","t":"The auditor of Kingfisher Cabinets, a nonissuer, tested an automated control over inventory costing in the 2023 audit with no exceptions. In 2024 the auditor used the 2023 evidence, performing inquiry with observation to confirm no changes. For 2025 the auditor wants to use the 2023 evidence again. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Kingfisher Cabinets, a nonissuer, tested an automated control over inventory costing in the 2023 audit with no exceptions. In 2024 the auditor used the 2023 evidence, performing inquiry with observation to confirm no changes. For 2025 the auditor wants to use the 2023 evidence again. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The control must be tested in the 2025 audit, because controls must be tested at least once in every third audit","The auditor must test every control the auditor relies on in every audit, without exception","The 2023 evidence can be used again in 2025 if there were no changes, because retesting is required only every fifth audit","The 2023 evidence can be used again in 2025 if the auditor makes inquiries of management only"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 330.13-.14","rat":"If evidence from a previous audit is used and there have been no changes, the control is tested at least once in every third audit.","ex":["If evidence from a previous audit is used and there have been no changes, the control is tested at least once in every third audit. The 2023 test means 2025 is the third audit.","The standard allows use of prior evidence, subject to the every-third-audit limit and other conditions.","The interval is every third audit, not every fifth.","Continuing relevance requires inquiry combined with observation or inspection, and testing at least every third audit; inquiry alone is not enough."],"wt":["","concept","limit","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Prior-audit control evidence may be used if still relevant, but controls must be tested at least once every third audit.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"19":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0004","t":"Which of the following is required when an auditor designs substantive analytical procedures for a nonissuer audit?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following is required when an auditor designs substantive analytical procedures for a nonissuer audit?","d":"","o":["Use the recorded amounts as the basis for the expectation, so that the comparison is consistent","Investigate every difference regardless of amount, since any difference may indicate a misstatement","Perform the procedure only after completing tests of details on the same assertion so results can be compared","Develop a precise expectation and determine the difference that is acceptable without further investigation"],"a":4,"cite":"AU-C 520.05; AU-C 520.07","rat":"The auditor develops a sufficiently precise expectation and determines the acceptable difference before comparing recorded amounts.","ex":["An expectation based on the recorded amounts is not independent and would not detect misstatements.","The auditor investigates differences that exceed the acceptable amount; investigation of every difference is not required.","Analytical procedures may be performed alone or with tests of details.","The auditor develops a sufficiently precise expectation and determines the acceptable difference before comparing recorded amounts."],"wt":["trap","limit","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Substantive analytics require a precise expectation, reliable data, and a preset acceptable difference.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"20":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0005","t":"Tanager Foods, a nonissuer, had 240 average employees in 2025 at an average salary of $52,000. Recorded wages are $13,080,000. The auditor's expectation is number of employees times average salary, and the auditor will investigate differences above $400,000. What is the appropriate action?","mc":{"s":"Tanager Foods, a nonissuer, had 240 average employees in 2025 at an average salary of $52,000. Recorded wages are $13,080,000. The auditor's expectation is number of employees times average salary, and the auditor will investigate differences above $400,000. What is the appropriate action?","d":"","o":["Investigate the $600,000 difference by inquiry and corroboration, because it exceeds the $400,000 threshold","Raise the threshold to $600,000 so that the difference does not require investigation or documentation","Take no further action, because the difference is under 5% of the recorded wages for the year","Reduce recorded wages by $600,000 to agree to the expectation"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 520.05d; AU-C 520.07","rat":"Expected wages are 240 x $52,000 = $12,480,000.","ex":["Expected wages are 240 x $52,000 = $12,480,000. The difference of $600,000 exceeds the threshold, so it must be investigated.","Changing the threshold after seeing the difference would defeat the purpose of setting it in advance.","The threshold is the auditor's predetermined amount of $400,000; the difference is 4.6% of recorded wages, but percentage is not the criterion.","The expectation is an estimate used to evaluate the recorded amount, not a basis for adjusting it."],"wt":["","trap","limit","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Investigate differences from expectation that exceed the predetermined acceptable amount.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Expected wages = 240 x 52,000 = 12,480,000","Difference = 13,080,000 - 12,480,000 = 600,000","600,000 > 400,000 threshold, so investigate"]}}],"21":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0006","t":"Near the end of the 2025 audit of Tanager Foods, a nonissuer, the auditor performs analytical procedures on the financial statements as a whole. What is their purpose?","mc":{"s":"Near the end of the 2025 audit of Tanager Foods, a nonissuer, the auditor performs analytical procedures on the financial statements as a whole. What is their purpose?","d":"","o":["To replace substantive procedures for accounts that the auditor has assessed as low risk of material misstatement","To help form an overall conclusion about consistency with the auditor's understanding of the entity","To determine the level of materiality for the financial statements as a whole","To identify whether individual transactions were recorded in the wrong period"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 520.06","rat":"Analytical procedures near the end of the audit help form an overall conclusion about consistency with the auditor's understanding.","ex":["Overall analytics do not replace substantive procedures.","Analytical procedures near the end of the audit help form an overall conclusion about consistency with the auditor's understanding.","Materiality is determined during planning and revised as the audit progresses, not by these procedures.","Testing individual transactions for cutoff is done through detailed procedures."],"wt":["trap","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"End-of-audit analytical procedures support the auditor's overall conclusion on the financial statements.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"22":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0007","t":"The auditor of Elmwood Hospitality, a nonissuer, plans a substantive analytical procedure on utilities expense using square footage data maintained by facilities staff in an unreviewed spreadsheet. No controls over the spreadsheet have been evaluated. What should the auditor do?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Elmwood Hospitality, a nonissuer, plans a substantive analytical procedure on utilities expense using square footage data maintained by facilities staff in an unreviewed spreadsheet. No controls over the spreadsheet have been evaluated. What should the auditor do?","d":"","o":["Use prior-year utilities expense as the expectation and treat the absence of a difference as sufficient","Widen the acceptable difference to allow for possible errors in the spreadsheet","Proceed with the analytics as planned, because analytical procedures are generally less sensitive to data quality than tests of details are","Evaluate the reliability of the data, including controls over its preparation, and obtain more evidence or change the procedure"],"a":4,"cite":"AU-C 520.05b","rat":"The auditor evaluates the reliability of the data behind the expectation and obtains further evidence or changes the procedure if it is not reliable.","ex":["Prior-year expense as expectation ignores changes and does not address the data problem.","Widening the threshold reduces the procedure's ability to detect misstatements rather than addressing reliability.","An expectation is only as good as its underlying data, so data quality matters for analytics.","The auditor evaluates the reliability of the data behind the expectation and obtains further evidence or changes the procedure if it is not reliable."],"wt":["trap","trap","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Evaluate the reliability of data used to develop an analytical expectation.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"23":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0008","t":"The auditor of Westfield Water Utility, a nonissuer, wants to use negative confirmations as the sole substantive procedure for 30,000 small residential receivable balances. Which condition must be present?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Westfield Water Utility, a nonissuer, wants to use negative confirmations as the sole substantive procedure for 30,000 small residential receivable balances. Which condition must be present?","d":"","o":["The auditor expects a high rate of exceptions, so that customers respond in large numbers to the requests","Management has asked the auditor not to contact customers directly","The auditor has assessed the risk as low and has evidence that relevant controls operate effectively","The balances are large and different from one another, so each needs individual attention"],"a":3,"cite":"AU-C 505.15","rat":"Negative confirmations alone are acceptable only when risk is low with supporting controls evidence, the population is many small homogeneous items, a very low exception rate is expected, and recipients will not disregard the requests.","ex":["The condition is a very low expected exception rate, not a high one.","A management request not to confirm is a refusal scenario, not a condition permitting negative confirmations.","Negative confirmations alone are acceptable only when risk is low with supporting controls evidence, the population is many small homogeneous items, a very low exception rate is expected, and recipients will not disregard the requests.","The condition is a large number of small, homogeneous balances."],"wt":["limit","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Negative confirmations are the sole procedure only for low risk, many small homogeneous items, and a very low expected exception rate.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"24":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0009","t":"Management of Redfield Cement, a nonissuer, asks the auditor not to confirm a $2.4 million receivable with the customer, saying the customer is in a dispute with the company. What should the auditor do?","mc":{"s":"Management of Redfield Cement, a nonissuer, asks the auditor not to confirm a $2.4 million receivable with the customer, saying the customer is in a dispute with the company. What should the auditor do?","d":"","o":["Comply with the request and rely on management's written representation about the balance owed by the customer as evidence","Send the confirmation anyway, using a customer contact provided by the company's sales staff","Inquire about the reasons, evaluate fraud risk implications, and perform alternative procedures to obtain evidence","Withdraw from the engagement immediately"],"a":3,"cite":"AU-C 505.08-.09","rat":"When management refuses, the auditor inquires, evaluates implications for risk including fraud, and performs alternative procedures.","ex":["A representation is not a substitute for alternative audit procedures.","Using a contact supplied by the company would not give the auditor control over the confirmation process.","When management refuses, the auditor inquires, evaluates implications for risk including fraud, and performs alternative procedures.","Withdrawal is not the first step; the auditor evaluates the refusal and considers alternative evidence."],"wt":["trap","trap","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"If management refuses to allow confirmation, inquire, evaluate fraud risk implications, and perform alternative procedures.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"25":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0010","t":"The auditor of Cypress Textiles, a nonissuer, sent positive confirmations to 60 customers. Twelve did not respond after a second request. The auditor has determined that a written response is not necessary to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence. What should the auditor do for each nonresponse?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Cypress Textiles, a nonissuer, sent positive confirmations to 60 customers. Twelve did not respond after a second request. The auditor has determined that a written response is not necessary to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence. What should the auditor do for each nonresponse?","d":"","o":["Send negative confirmations to the same customers as a substitute","Perform alternative procedures, such as examining subsequent cash receipts and shipping documents","Treat the nonresponse as confirmation that the recorded balance is correct, because silence implies agreement","Treat each nonresponse as a misstatement and project the total to the population"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 505.12","rat":"For each nonresponse the auditor performs alternative procedures to obtain relevant and reliable evidence.","ex":["Negative confirmations provide less persuasive evidence and would not substitute for alternative procedures.","For each nonresponse the auditor performs alternative procedures to obtain relevant and reliable evidence.","Silence in response to a positive confirmation is not evidence that the balance is correct.","A nonresponse is not by itself a misstatement; if a written response were essential, a different treatment would apply."],"wt":["concept","","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Perform alternative procedures for each nonresponse to a positive confirmation request.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"26":[{"i":"AUD-III-D-0011","t":"The auditor of Merrimack Steel, a nonissuer, receives a customer confirmation reply by email from a generic address, not from the confirming party's known domain, and the reply agrees with the recorded balance exactly. The auditor has doubts about its reliability. What is the most appropriate response?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Merrimack Steel, a nonissuer, receives a customer confirmation reply by email from a generic address, not from the confirming party's known domain, and the reply agrees with the recorded balance exactly. The auditor has doubts about its reliability. What is the most appropriate response?","d":"","o":["Get further evidence, such as calling the customer at an independently verified number","Accept the reply as reliable, because it agrees with the recorded balance and came back promptly","Ask the sales manager to confirm that the customer sent the reply, and file the manager's confirmation","Treat the reply as an exception and propose an adjustment for the full amount of the customer's balance"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 505.10-.11","rat":"When factors give rise to doubt about reliability, the auditor obtains further evidence to resolve it and considers implications for risk if the response is unreliable.","ex":["When factors give rise to doubt about reliability, the auditor obtains further evidence to resolve it and considers implications for risk if the response is unreliable.","Agreement with the recorded balance does not make an unverified response reliable.","Asking company staff to authenticate the response does not provide independent evidence.","Doubt about reliability is not the same as an exception, and no misstatement has been shown."],"wt":["","trap","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Evaluation","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Resolve doubts about the reliability of confirmation responses with further evidence.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"27":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0001","t":"The auditor of Lakemont Insurance, a nonissuer, is responding to assessed risks of material misstatement for an accounting estimate. Which set of approaches may the auditor use?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Lakemont Insurance, a nonissuer, is responding to assessed risks of material misstatement for an accounting estimate. Which set of approaches may the auditor use?","d":"","o":["Test only the arithmetical accuracy of management's calculation","Rely on the prior year's audit conclusion if the estimation method has not changed","Request a representation that the estimate is reasonable and limit procedures to the representation","Use events up to the report date, test how management made the estimate, or develop an auditor's range"],"a":4,"cite":"AU-C 540.17","rat":"The further audit procedures for estimates include one or more of these three approaches.","ex":["Arithmetical accuracy alone does not address methods, assumptions or data.","Prior-year conclusions do not establish that the current-year estimate is free from misstatement.","A representation is not sufficient evidence when the risk is assessed, and does not respond to the risk.","The further audit procedures for estimates include one or more of these three approaches."],"wt":["trap","trap","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Responses to estimate risks: events to the report date, testing management's process, or an auditor's estimate or range.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"28":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0002","t":"Management of Pelham Bank, a nonissuer, records a loan loss allowance of $4,600,000. The auditor develops a range of $3,400,000 to $4,200,000, with each amount in the range supported by sufficient appropriate evidence. What is the misstatement?","mc":{"s":"Management of Pelham Bank, a nonissuer, records a loan loss allowance of $4,600,000. The auditor develops a range of $3,400,000 to $4,200,000, with each amount in the range supported by sufficient appropriate evidence. What is the misstatement?","d":"","o":["$0","$400,000","$800,000","$1,200,000"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 540.A139","rat":"When management's estimate is outside the auditor's range, the misstatement is the difference between the estimate and the nearest point of the range: $4,600,000 - $4,200,000.","ex":["The estimate is outside the range, so a misstatement exists.","When management's estimate is outside the auditor's range, the misstatement is the difference between the estimate and the nearest point of the range: $4,600,000 - $4,200,000.","This measures the difference from the midpoint of the range ($3,800,000), which is not the required measure.","This measures the difference from the low end of the range, the point farthest from management's estimate."],"wt":["concept","","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Evaluation","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"If management's estimate is outside the auditor's range, the misstatement is measured to the nearest end of the range.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Management estimate 4,600,000 is above the range 3,400,000 to 4,200,000","Misstatement = 4,600,000 - 4,200,000 = 400,000"]}}],"29":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0003","t":"The auditor of Quarry Hill Foods, a nonissuer, plans to use pension obligation figures prepared by an actuary that management engaged. What must the auditor do to the extent necessary, given the significance of the specialist's work?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Quarry Hill Foods, a nonissuer, plans to use pension obligation figures prepared by an actuary that management engaged. What must the auditor do to the extent necessary, given the significance of the specialist's work?","d":"","o":["Obtain a written representation from management that the actuary is qualified and that the figures are accurate","Evaluate the specialist's competence, capabilities and objectivity, and the appropriateness of the work","Refer to the actuary in the auditor's report to make clear that the figures came from an outside specialist","Accept the figures because the actuary is independent of the auditor's firm and holds a professional credential"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 501.27","rat":"The auditor evaluates the management's specialist's competence, capabilities and objectivity, understands the work, and evaluates its appropriateness.","ex":["A representation does not replace the auditor's own evaluation.","The auditor evaluates the management's specialist's competence, capabilities and objectivity, understands the work, and evaluates its appropriateness.","The auditor does not refer to a specialist in an unmodified report.","Independence from the auditor's firm is not the test; the specialist is engaged by management and the auditor must evaluate objectivity."],"wt":["trap","","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Evaluate a management's specialist's competence, capabilities and objectivity, and the appropriateness of the work.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"30":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0004","t":"Sable Holdings, a nonissuer, has $6.2 million of marketable securities held by a broker on its behalf. Which evidence about existence is most reliable?","mc":{"s":"Sable Holdings, a nonissuer, has $6.2 million of marketable securities held by a broker on its behalf. Which evidence about existence is most reliable?","d":"","o":["The auditor's recomputation of unrealized gains using year-end quoted prices","The broker's monthly statement supplied by the treasurer","Minutes of the investment committee approving the purchases","A confirmation the auditor receives directly from the broker"],"a":4,"cite":"AU-C 500.A22; AU-C 505.07","rat":"Confirmation obtained directly from the external custodian is the most reliable evidence of existence.","ex":["Recomputing gains tests valuation, not existence.","A statement supplied by the client's treasurer is obtained indirectly and is less reliable than a direct confirmation.","Approval of purchases does not show that the securities are still held.","Confirmation obtained directly from the external custodian is the most reliable evidence of existence."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"External confirmation from the custodian is the most reliable evidence of existence for securities.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"31":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0005","t":"Halcyon Corp., a nonissuer, carries a 12% investment in a privately held company at an amount based on the investee's financial results. The investee's financial statements are unaudited, and the investment is material. What should the auditor do?","mc":{"s":"Halcyon Corp., a nonissuer, carries a 12% investment in a privately held company at an amount based on the investee's financial results. The investee's financial statements are unaudited, and the investment is material. What should the auditor do?","d":"","o":["Accept management's valuation, because a minority investment does not require the auditor to obtain evidence about the investee's results","Read the unaudited statements and accept them if they are signed by the investee's chief executive officer and chief financial officer","Apply, or have another auditor apply, appropriate procedures to the investee's statements, and consider the opinion if evidence is lacking","Modify the opinion at once, without performing any further procedures on the investee's financial results or asking management for support"],"a":3,"cite":"AU-C 501.05","rat":"When the investee is unaudited, the auditor applies procedures to its statements or arranges for another auditor to, and modifies the opinion if unable to get sufficient evidence.","ex":["The valuation is based on the investee's results, so evidence about those results is needed regardless of ownership percentage.","Signature by the investee's chief executive does not provide audit evidence about the investee's results.","When the investee is unaudited, the auditor applies procedures to its statements or arranges for another auditor to, and modifies the opinion if unable to get sufficient evidence.","The auditor first tries to obtain evidence; modification follows only if the auditor cannot."],"wt":["trap","trap","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Obtain evidence about an investee's financial results, including procedures on unaudited statements, before concluding on opinion effects.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"32":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0006","t":"Inventory is material to Keystone Hardware, a nonissuer. Severe weather closes the warehouse on the count date, and the auditor cannot attend the count. What should the auditor do?","mc":{"s":"Inventory is material to Keystone Hardware, a nonissuer. Severe weather closes the warehouse on the count date, and the auditor cannot attend the count. What should the auditor do?","d":"","o":["Make or observe some counts on an alternative date and test the intervening transactions","Disclaim an opinion immediately, because attendance at the count is mandatory in all circumstances","Rely on the perpetual inventory records and obtain a management representation about quantities","Ask management to recount the inventory and rely on its results alone"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 501.14","rat":"When attendance is prevented by unforeseen circumstances, the auditor makes or observes some counts on an alternative date and tests intervening transactions.","ex":["When attendance is prevented by unforeseen circumstances, the auditor makes or observes some counts on an alternative date and tests intervening transactions.","Alternative procedures come first; a modified opinion applies only if sufficient evidence cannot be obtained.","Records and representations do not provide evidence of existence and condition of the inventory.","Management's recount without the auditor's observation does not give the auditor direct evidence."],"wt":["","trap","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"If unable to attend a count for unforeseen reasons, make or observe counts on another date and test intervening transactions.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"33":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0007","t":"While observing the physical inventory of Tidewater Lumber, a nonissuer, the auditor wants to test both the existence and the completeness of the count records. Which test counts are most appropriate?","mc":{"s":"While observing the physical inventory of Tidewater Lumber, a nonissuer, the auditor wants to test both the existence and the completeness of the count records. Which test counts are most appropriate?","d":"","o":["Select items from the count sheets only and locate them in the warehouse, testing existence but not completeness","Select items in the warehouse only, and trace them to the perpetual records","Trace items from the count sheets to the warehouse, and items from the warehouse to the count sheets","Select items from the perpetual records and trace them to the count sheets"],"a":3,"cite":"AU-C 501.12a(iv)","rat":"Tracing from records to the floor tests existence, and tracing from the floor to the records tests completeness, so both directions are needed.","ex":["This tests existence only and would not detect items on the floor that were omitted from the count.","This ignores the count sheets, which are the record of what was counted.","Tracing from records to the floor tests existence, and tracing from the floor to the records tests completeness, so both directions are needed.","The perpetual records are not the count records, and this does not test the physical inventory itself."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Test counts from records to floor test existence; floor to records test completeness.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"34":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0008","t":"Mosaic Foods, an issuer, keeps 30% of its inventory in a public warehouse, which is a significant proportion of its assets. Which procedures are appropriate under PCAOB standards?","mc":{"s":"Mosaic Foods, an issuer, keeps 30% of its inventory in a public warehouse, which is a significant proportion of its assets. Which procedures are appropriate under PCAOB standards?","d":"","o":["Rely on the warehouse receipts held by the company, because they evidence title to the goods stored there","Confirm directly in writing with the warehouse and apply further procedures, such as observing counts","Obtain a management representation about quantities held, since the warehouse is independent","Perform no procedures, because inventory held by a third party is outside the scope of the audit"],"a":2,"cite":"PCAOB AS 2510.14; AU-C 501.16","rat":"The auditor ordinarily obtains direct written confirmation from the custodian and, for significant amounts, applies further procedures.","ex":["Company-held receipts do not give the auditor direct evidence that the goods are on hand.","The auditor ordinarily obtains direct written confirmation from the custodian and, for significant amounts, applies further procedures.","A representation does not substitute for evidence from the custodian.","Inventory held by third parties is part of the company's assets and must be audited."],"wt":["trap","","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"For inventory at public warehouses, confirm with the custodian and apply additional procedures if significant.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"35":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0009","t":"The auditor of Bristow Tools, a nonissuer, has identified pending litigation that may give rise to a risk of material misstatement. Which is required?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Bristow Tools, a nonissuer, has identified pending litigation that may give rise to a risk of material misstatement. Which is required?","d":"","o":["Rely on management's description of the litigation and its evaluation, since contact with counsel is optional","Ask legal counsel to respond about all matters of any size, however small","Have management send the inquiry to its lawyer and forward the response to the auditor","Send counsel a letter of inquiry, prepared by management, and seek a direct response from external counsel"],"a":4,"cite":"AU-C 501.19; AU-C 501.23","rat":"The auditor seeks direct communication with external counsel through a letter prepared by management and sent by the auditor.","ex":["Direct communication with counsel is required unless procedures indicate no litigation exists.","The inquiry covers matters that management considers material, not every matter of any size.","The auditor must send the letter and receive the response directly to keep control over the process.","The auditor seeks direct communication with external counsel through a letter prepared by management and sent by the auditor."],"wt":["trap","limit","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Seek direct communication with external legal counsel by an inquiry letter prepared by management and sent by the auditor.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"36":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0010","t":"Management of Fairview Homes, a nonissuer, refuses to allow the auditor to communicate with the external lawyer handling a significant lawsuit. Management offers the in-house counsel's written summary instead. Which conclusion is correct?","mc":{"s":"Management of Fairview Homes, a nonissuer, refuses to allow the auditor to communicate with the external lawyer handling a significant lawsuit. Management offers the in-house counsel's written summary instead. Which conclusion is correct?","d":"","o":["The auditor should modify the opinion, because management refused permission to communicate with external legal counsel","The auditor should modify the opinion only if the in-house counsel's summary is inconsistent with the financial statements","The auditor should rely on management's written assessment of the lawsuit and consider only the adequacy of disclosure","The in-house counsel's summary is an acceptable substitute, so no modification is needed"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 501.20; AU-C 501.25b","rat":"If management refuses permission to communicate with external counsel, the auditor modifies the opinion.","ex":["If management refuses permission to communicate with external counsel, the auditor modifies the opinion.","The refusal by itself requires the modification, regardless of what the summary says.","Management's own assessment does not replace evidence from external counsel.","Evidence from in-house counsel is not a substitute for direct communication with external counsel."],"wt":["","concept","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Evaluation","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Management's refusal to permit communication with external counsel requires a modified opinion.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"37":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0011","t":"In an audit of an issuer, the client's lawyer responds to the inquiry letter that the outcome of a pending lawsuit cannot be estimated because of inherent uncertainties, and the lawyer otherwise responds fully. How should the auditor view this?","mc":{"s":"In an audit of an issuer, the client's lawyer responds to the inquiry letter that the outcome of a pending lawsuit cannot be estimated because of inherent uncertainties, and the lawyer otherwise responds fully. How should the auditor view this?","d":"","o":["As a reason to require the lawyer to provide an estimate before the report can be issued","As a limitation on the scope of the audit that precludes an unqualified opinion, the same as a refusal to respond","As an uncertainty about a future outcome affecting the statements, whose effect on the report the auditor considers","As evidence that the lawsuit is not likely to result in an unfavorable outcome"],"a":3,"cite":"PCAOB AS 2505.13-.14","rat":"A lawyer's inability to estimate due to inherent uncertainty is treated as an uncertainty affecting the statements, not a scope limitation.","ex":["Lawyers may not be able to form a conclusion, and the auditor cannot require an estimate.","A lawyer's refusal to furnish information is a scope limitation; an inability to estimate is different.","A lawyer's inability to estimate due to inherent uncertainty is treated as an uncertainty affecting the statements, not a scope limitation.","The response gives no assurance about the likelihood of an unfavorable outcome."],"wt":["trap","concept","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A lawyer's inability to estimate the outcome is an uncertainty, not a scope limitation; a refusal to respond is a scope limitation.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"38":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0012","t":"For which period does the auditor of a nonissuer evaluate whether substantial doubt exists about the entity's ability to continue as a going concern under AICPA standards?","mc":{"s":"For which period does the auditor of a nonissuer evaluate whether substantial doubt exists about the entity's ability to continue as a going concern under AICPA standards?","d":"","o":["The period the framework requires or, if none, one year after issuance or availability of the statements","Only through the date of the auditor's report","Six months after the date of the auditor's report, regardless of the framework's requirements","Twelve months from the balance sheet date only, whatever period the financial reporting framework requires"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 570.12 and definition of reasonable period of time","rat":"A reasonable period is the period the framework requires or, if none, one year after the financial statements are issued or available to be issued.","ex":["A reasonable period is the period the framework requires or, if none, one year after the financial statements are issued or available to be issued.","The evaluation looks forward beyond the report date, not only through it.","There is no six-month period in the standard.","The default measurement date is the issuance (or availability) date, not the balance sheet date."],"wt":["","concept","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"AICPA reasonable period: as the framework requires, otherwise one year after the financial statements are issued or available to be issued.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"39":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0013","t":"Under PCAOB standards, over what period does the auditor of an issuer evaluate whether there is substantial doubt about the entity's ability to continue as a going concern?","mc":{"s":"Under PCAOB standards, over what period does the auditor of an issuer evaluate whether there is substantial doubt about the entity's ability to continue as a going concern?","d":"","o":["Two years after the date of the financial statements being audited by the firm","One year after the date the financial statements are issued, as under the AICPA standard for nonissuers","Only through the date of the auditor's report","A reasonable period not exceeding one year beyond the date of the financial statements"],"a":4,"cite":"PCAOB AS 2415.02","rat":"PCAOB standards use a period not to exceed one year beyond the date of the financial statements being audited.","ex":["No two-year evaluation period exists.","The one-year-after-issuance period is from the AICPA standard, not the PCAOB standard.","The evaluation looks beyond the report date, not only through it.","PCAOB standards use a period not to exceed one year beyond the date of the financial statements being audited."],"wt":["limit","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"PCAOB: a reasonable period not to exceed one year beyond the balance sheet date.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"40":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0014","t":"The auditor of Harborview Marine, a nonissuer, finds substantial doubt about its ability to continue as a going concern. Management's plan depends on $1.5 million in funding from the principal owner. The owner gives verbal assurances to the auditor but declines to provide anything in writing. What is the appropriate conclusion?","mc":{"s":"The auditor of Harborview Marine, a nonissuer, finds substantial doubt about its ability to continue as a going concern. Management's plan depends on $1.5 million in funding from the principal owner. The owner gives verbal assurances to the auditor but declines to provide anything in writing. What is the appropriate conclusion?","d":"","o":["Verbal assurance from the owner is sufficient, because the owner has direct knowledge of the owner's own intent and means","The plans are insufficient to alleviate substantial doubt, because written evidence of the owner's intent is missing","Substantial doubt is alleviated if the owner's financial statements show the ability to provide the funds","A management representation about the owner's intent is adequate support for the plan"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 570.17","rat":"Failure to obtain written evidence of the supporting party's intent means the plans are insufficient to alleviate substantial doubt.","ex":["Written evidence of intent is required, and oral assurances do not meet the requirement.","Failure to obtain written evidence of the supporting party's intent means the plans are insufficient to alleviate substantial doubt.","Ability to provide funds is not enough; the intent to do so must also be evidenced in writing.","A management representation does not substitute for the owner's written evidence of intent."],"wt":["trap","","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Evaluation","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Written evidence of a supporting party's intent to provide funds is required before its support can alleviate substantial doubt.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"41":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0015","t":"In a single audit of Fairhaven School District, what must the auditor determine about compliance for each major program?","mc":{"s":"In a single audit of Fairhaven School District, what must the auditor determine about compliance for each major program?","d":"","o":["Whether the auditee complied with all federal requirements for every federal program, major or not","Whether the auditee complied only with the requirements the auditee itself considers significant","Compliance with requirements that may have a direct and material effect on each major program","Whether the auditee's financial statements comply with the compliance supplement"],"a":3,"cite":"2 CFR 200.514(d)","rat":"The auditor determines compliance with requirements that may have a direct and material effect on each major program, using the compliance supplement.","ex":["Compliance testing is directed at major programs, not every program.","The auditor, not the auditee, determines which requirements to test.","The auditor determines compliance with requirements that may have a direct and material effect on each major program, using the compliance supplement.","The financial statements are audited under GAAP; the compliance supplement addresses program requirements."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Test compliance with requirements that may have a direct and material effect on each major program.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"42":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0016","t":"In a single audit of Cedar County, the auditor finds a deficiency in controls over eligibility for a major program. In relation to what does the auditor determine whether it is a significant deficiency or material weakness for reporting an audit finding?","mc":{"s":"In a single audit of Cedar County, the auditor finds a deficiency in controls over eligibility for a major program. In relation to what does the auditor determine whether it is a significant deficiency or material weakness for reporting an audit finding?","d":"","o":["Total federal awards expended by the county","Materiality for the county's financial statements as a whole, as used in the financial statement audit","The county's overall system of internal control across all departments","A type of compliance requirement for the major program identified in the compliance supplement"],"a":4,"cite":"2 CFR 200.516(a)(1)","rat":"For audit findings, severity is determined in relation to a type of compliance requirement for a major program.","ex":["Total awards expended is used to set Type A thresholds, not to judge severity of deficiencies.","The basis is the compliance requirement for the major program, not financial statement materiality.","The evaluation is for controls over major programs, not the entire system.","For audit findings, severity is determined in relation to a type of compliance requirement for a major program."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Deficiency severity in a single audit is judged in relation to a type of compliance requirement for a major program.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"43":[{"i":"AUD-III-E-0017","t":"In testing allowable costs for a major program at Dover Housing Authority, the auditor identifies known questioned costs of $12,000 and projects likely questioned costs of $31,000 for that type of compliance requirement. What must the auditor do?","mc":{"s":"In testing allowable costs for a major program at Dover Housing Authority, the auditor identifies known questioned costs of $12,000 and projects likely questioned costs of $31,000 for that type of compliance requirement. What must the auditor do?","d":"","o":["Report a finding with known questioned costs of $12,000, because likely costs exceed $25,000","Report a finding only if both known and likely questioned costs exceed $25,000","Report a finding that states known questioned costs of $31,000 for the compliance requirement","Report no finding, because the known questioned costs are below the $25,000 reporting threshold"],"a":1,"cite":"2 CFR 200.516(a)(3)","rat":"A finding is required when either known or likely questioned costs exceed $25,000 for a type of compliance requirement for a major program, and it reports the known costs.","ex":["A finding is required when either known or likely questioned costs exceed $25,000 for a type of compliance requirement for a major program, and it reports the known costs.","Either known or likely questioned costs above $25,000 are sufficient.","The finding reports known questioned costs; the likely amount is a projection and is not reported as known.","The $25,000 test applies to either known or likely questioned costs, so likely costs of $31,000 trigger a finding."],"wt":["","limit","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Report a finding when known or likely questioned costs exceed $25,000 for a compliance requirement of a major program.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Known questioned costs 12,000 (below 25,000); likely 31,000 (above 25,000)","Either exceeding 25,000 triggers a finding"]}}],"44":[{"i":"AUD-III-F-0001","t":"Which statement about misstatements that are clearly trivial is correct under AICPA standards?","mc":{"s":"Which statement about misstatements that are clearly trivial is correct under AICPA standards?","d":"","o":["When it is uncertain whether an item is clearly trivial, it is treated as clearly trivial","Clearly trivial is not another way to say not material; such items are of a much smaller magnitude","Clearly trivial means any misstatement below materiality for the financial statements as a whole","Clearly trivial misstatements must be accumulated and communicated the same way as all other misstatements"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 450.05; AU-C 450.A2","rat":"Clearly trivial is a much smaller order of magnitude than materiality, and it is not just a synonym for not material.","ex":["When there is uncertainty, the item is considered not clearly trivial.","Clearly trivial is a much smaller order of magnitude than materiality, and it is not just a synonym for not material.","Below-materiality misstatements are not automatically clearly trivial and must still be accumulated.","The auditor accumulates misstatements other than those that are clearly trivial."],"wt":["trap","","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Clearly trivial misstatements are far smaller than materiality; when uncertain, treat the item as not clearly trivial.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"45":[{"i":"AUD-III-F-0002","t":"Materiality for the audit of Ridgeway Steel, a nonissuer, is $200,000. Uncorrected misstatements that each overstate pretax income are a $45,000 factual cutoff error, a $70,000 judgmental difference in the allowance, and $55,000 projected from a receivables sample. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","mc":{"s":"Materiality for the audit of Ridgeway Steel, a nonissuer, is $200,000. Uncorrected misstatements that each overstate pretax income are a $45,000 factual cutoff error, a $70,000 judgmental difference in the allowance, and $55,000 projected from a receivables sample. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","d":"","o":["The $170,000 aggregate approaches materiality, so consider revising the plan and request correction","The projected $55,000 should be excluded, because a projected amount is an estimate and not a misstatement","The financial statements are materially misstated, because the aggregate exceeds performance materiality of $150,000","The $170,000 aggregate is below materiality, so no further action is required"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 450.06b; AU-C 450.07; AU-C 450.A6","rat":"The total is $45,000 + $70,000 + $55,000 = $170,000, which is 85% of materiality.","ex":["The total is $45,000 + $70,000 + $55,000 = $170,000, which is 85% of materiality. The auditor considers revising the plan and requests correction.","Projected misstatements are the auditor's best estimate and are evaluated with the others.","The comparison for material misstatement is with materiality, not performance materiality.","When the aggregate approaches materiality, the auditor must consider whether the strategy and plan need revision, and being below materiality does not end the analysis."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"When aggregated misstatements approach materiality, reconsider the audit strategy and plan and request correction.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total = 45,000 + 70,000 + 55,000 = 170,000","170,000 / 200,000 = 85% of materiality"]}}],"46":[{"i":"AUD-III-F-0003","t":"Which describes a significant deficiency in internal control under AICPA standards?","mc":{"s":"Which describes a significant deficiency in internal control under AICPA standards?","d":"","o":["Any control failure that causes a misstatement below the level of clearly trivial","A deficiency less severe than a material weakness yet important enough to merit attention by those charged with governance","A deficiency that results in a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement will not be prevented, or detected and corrected, on a timely basis","A deficiency in an application control that management has already remediated before year-end"],"a":2,"cite":"AU-C 265.07","rat":"This is the definition of a significant deficiency.","ex":["Size of a resulting misstatement is not the definition, and clearly trivial amounts are not the test.","This is the definition of a significant deficiency.","That describes a material weakness, which is more severe.","Remediation does not change the classification; remediated significant deficiencies are still communicated."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A significant deficiency is less severe than a material weakness but merits the attention of those charged with governance.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"47":[{"i":"AUD-III-F-0004","t":"In an integrated audit of an issuer, which finding is an indicator of a material weakness in internal control over financial reporting?","mc":{"s":"In an integrated audit of an issuer, which finding is an indicator of a material weakness in internal control over financial reporting?","d":"","o":["A restatement of previously issued financial statements to reflect a change in accounting principle","Auditor identification of an immaterial misstatement the controls would have detected","Identification of fraud, whether or not material, on the part of senior management","A single deficiency in a control over a low-risk account that has an effective compensating control"],"a":3,"cite":"PCAOB AS 2201.69","rat":"Fraud by senior management, whether or not material, is an indicator of a material weakness.","ex":["The indicator is a restatement to correct a material misstatement, not one for a change in principle.","The indicator is a material misstatement that the controls would not have detected, not an immaterial one that they would have.","Fraud by senior management, whether or not material, is an indicator of a material weakness.","A deficiency with an effective compensating control on a low-risk account is unlikely to be a material weakness."],"wt":["trap","trap","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Material weakness indicators include senior management fraud, restatements for errors, and ineffective audit committee oversight.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"48":[{"i":"AUD-III-G-0001","t":"Which representation must an auditor of a nonissuer request from management?","mc":{"s":"Which representation must an auditor of a nonissuer request from management?","d":"","o":["That the audit was conducted in accordance with GAAS and that the evidence obtained was sufficient for the opinion","That management will not dispute the auditor's opinion when it is issued","That management has no obligation to pay the auditor's fee if the opinion is modified","That management provided all relevant information and access, and that all transactions have been recorded"],"a":4,"cite":"AU-C 580.10-.11","rat":"Management must represent that it provided all relevant information and access, and that all transactions were recorded.","ex":["Sufficiency of evidence is the auditor's own conclusion, not a representation from management.","Management cannot be asked to waive the right to disagree with the opinion.","Fee arrangements are terms of the engagement, not required representations.","Management must represent that it provided all relevant information and access, and that all transactions were recorded."],"wt":["trap","trap","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Required representations cover management's responsibilities, information provided, and completeness of transactions.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"49":[{"i":"AUD-III-G-0002","t":"Management of Silverline Foods, a nonissuer, refuses to sign the representation that it has fulfilled its responsibility for the preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements and for internal control. What should the auditor do?","mc":{"s":"Management of Silverline Foods, a nonissuer, refuses to sign the representation that it has fulfilled its responsibility for the preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements and for internal control. What should the auditor do?","d":"","o":["Disclaim an opinion on the financial statements or withdraw from the engagement","Perform additional substantive procedures to replace the representation","Accept a letter from management's attorney describing the responsibilities instead","Issue a qualified opinion with a scope limitation paragraph"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 580.25b","rat":"If management does not provide the required representations about its responsibilities, the auditor disclaims an opinion or withdraws.","ex":["If management does not provide the required representations about its responsibilities, the auditor disclaims an opinion or withdraws.","Other audit evidence cannot replace these representations.","An attorney's letter is not a written representation from management about its own responsibilities.","The standard specifies a disclaimer or withdrawal, not a qualified opinion."],"wt":["","trap","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Evaluation","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"If management will not give the representations about its responsibilities, disclaim an opinion or withdraw.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"50":[{"i":"AUD-III-G-0003","t":"In the audit of Argent Semiconductor, an issuer, which is appropriate for the management representation letter under PCAOB standards?","mc":{"s":"In the audit of Argent Semiconductor, an issuer, which is appropriate for the management representation letter under PCAOB standards?","d":"","o":["Signed by the controller and dated as of the start of fieldwork","Signed by senior management, normally the CEO and CFO, and dated as of the date of the auditor's report","Signed by the CFO only and dated the day the financial statements are approved by the board","Signed by the audit committee chair only and dated as of the balance sheet date of the statements audited"],"a":2,"cite":"PCAOB AS 2805.09","rat":"The letter is signed by those with overall responsibility, normally the CEO and CFO, and is made as of the date of the auditor's report.","ex":["The controller alone is not the normally required signer, and the date must be the report date, not fieldwork start.","The letter is signed by those with overall responsibility, normally the CEO and CFO, and is made as of the date of the auditor's report.","The date must be the auditor's report date, and the letter normally includes the CEO as well as the CFO.","Representations come from management, not the audit committee chair, and are dated as of the report date."],"wt":["limit","","limit","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Issuer representation letters are signed by senior management (normally CEO and CFO) and dated as of the auditor's report.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"51":[{"i":"AUD-III-H-0001","t":"Dulcet Retail's financial statements are for the year ended December 31, 2025, and the auditor's report will be dated February 20, 2026. On January 25, 2026, its largest customer files for bankruptcy, and the receivable at year-end had been deteriorating during 2025. How should the event be treated?","mc":{"s":"Dulcet Retail's financial statements are for the year ended December 31, 2025, and the auditor's report will be dated February 20, 2026. On January 25, 2026, its largest customer files for bankruptcy, and the receivable at year-end had been deteriorating during 2025. How should the event be treated?","d":"","o":["As a matter outside the audit, because it occurred after the balance sheet date","As an event requiring adjustment only if the bankruptcy had been filed before December 31, 2025","As an event about conditions that arose only after year-end, requiring disclosure without any adjustment","As evidence about conditions existing at year-end, requiring an adjustment to the allowance for credit losses"],"a":4,"cite":"PCAOB AS 2801.03-.05","rat":"A customer's bankruptcy after year-end that reflects deterioration existing at year-end gives additional evidence about conditions at the balance sheet date and requires adjustment.","ex":["Events through the date of the auditor's report are within the auditor's subsequent events procedures.","The filing date is not the test; the underlying condition existed before year-end.","Nonrecognized events relate to conditions that did not exist at the balance sheet date, which is not the situation described.","A customer's bankruptcy after year-end that reflects deterioration existing at year-end gives additional evidence about conditions at the balance sheet date and requires adjustment."],"wt":["trap","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"H","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Events that give evidence about conditions existing at the balance sheet date require adjustment; others require disclosure only.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"52":[{"i":"AUD-III-H-0002","t":"Which procedures should the auditor of a nonissuer perform to identify subsequent events for the period from the balance sheet date to the date of the auditor's report?","mc":{"s":"Which procedures should the auditor of a nonissuer perform to identify subsequent events for the period from the balance sheet date to the date of the auditor's report?","d":"","o":["Reperform the year-end closing procedures for every month in the subsequent period after the balance sheet date","Reconfirm every year-end balance with the original confirming parties as of the report date","Inquire of management and governance, read minutes of later meetings, and read the latest interim statements","Send inquiry letters to all customers and suppliers asking about events since year-end"],"a":3,"cite":"AU-C 560.10","rat":"The procedures include inquiring, reading later minutes, and reading the latest interim statements.","ex":["Reperforming closing procedures does not address events after year-end.","Reconfirming every balance is not required and would not identify subsequent events.","The procedures include inquiring, reading later minutes, and reading the latest interim statements.","Broad inquiry letters to all customers and suppliers are not among the required procedures."],"wt":["trap","trap","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"H","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Subsequent event procedures: understand management's process, inquire, read later minutes, and read the latest interim statements.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"53":[{"i":"AUD-III-H-0003","t":"Two months after the auditor released a report on the 2025 financial statements of Neptune Shipping, a nonissuer, the auditor learns that a warranty reserve was materially understated at the report date. Management does not want to revise the statements, which lenders have already received. What should the auditor do?","mc":{"s":"Two months after the auditor released a report on the 2025 financial statements of Neptune Shipping, a nonissuer, the auditor learns that a warranty reserve was materially understated at the report date. Management does not want to revise the statements, which lenders have already received. What should the auditor do?","d":"","o":["Discuss it with management and governance and, if not revised, act to prevent reliance on the report","Take no action, because the auditor has no further responsibility once the report has been released","Add a note to the working papers and consider the matter when planning the next year's audit","Withdraw the report by notifying the lenders directly without first informing management or governance"],"a":1,"cite":"AU-C 560.15-.18","rat":"The auditor discusses the matter, and if management does not revise and take steps to inform third parties, notifies management and governance and takes appropriate action to prevent reliance.","ex":["The auditor discusses the matter, and if management does not revise and take steps to inform third parties, notifies management and governance and takes appropriate action to prevent reliance.","The auditor has responsibilities for facts that existed at the report date and become known after release.","Deferring the matter to next year's audit would leave the users relying on the report.","The auditor first works with management and those charged with governance before seeking to prevent reliance."],"wt":["","trap","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"H","skill":"Evaluation","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"For facts discovered after release, discuss with management, seek revision, and if needed act to prevent reliance on the report.","from":"2026-01-01"}}]};
window.PAIRS=[];
